Q: How to check if Current Hour falls between certain range A: <p>Yes that is a valid approach.</p>
<p>Here is code using the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs/api/java.base/java/time/package-summary.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">java.time</a> classes built into Java 8 and later.</p>
<pre><code>import java.time.LocalTime;
...
int hour = LocalTime.now().getHour();
</code></pre>
<p>It's a bit shorter to get the hour. There is also an overloaded <code>now(ZoneId zoneId)</code> method.</p>
<p>Or make it a one liner with a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs//api/java.base/java/time/temporal/TemporalQuery.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">temporal query</a> passed to <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/docs//api/java.base/java/time/LocalTime.html#query(java.time.temporal.TemporalQuery)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>LocalTime#query</code></a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Boolean isBetween7and10AM = 
    LocalTime
    .now()
    .query( date -&gt; 
        date.get(ChronoField.CLOCK_HOUR_OF_DAY) &gt;= 7 
        &amp;&amp; date.get(ChronoField.CLOCK_HOUR_OF_DAY) &lt; 10
    );
</code></pre>
